  "course_name": "Deep Learning",
  "course_description": "A condensed, two-day introduction to Deep Learning for Master of Data Science students about to start their theses. Day 1 builds the foundations (what makes Deep Learning different, the training loop and backpropagation, making training work, convolutions and vision). Day 2 goes from sequences to attention and transformers, to pretrained models and LLMs in practice.",
  "student_console": false,
  "semester_start": "2026-11-21",
  "semester_end": "2026-11-28",
  "generated_at": "2026-11-18T15:17:39+00:00",
  "timezone": "Europe/Berlin",
  "archive_datetime": "2027-01-31",
  "home_markdown": "**Welcome to the Deep Learning Workshop for 2026.**\n\nWe meet on two Saturdays, **21 and 28 November, 10:00-18:00**, in **Room 3.30, Hertie School (Friedrichstraße 180)**. Each day has approximately three sessions: 10:00-12:30, 13:30-15:00 and 15:30-18:00 - these are not immutably fixed times; some sessions are longer, some shorter - and we'll take breaks as needed.\n\nTo get the materials, join the course from the **Join** page: it takes a minute and you need a GitHub account.\n\n---\n\nThe sessions are envisioned as **interactive lectures**, with primarily theoretical content being delivered on the day. Additionally, I will provide **handouts** and **notebooks** for further, more practical (i.e. code-implemented) self-study. The theoretical in-person sessions should set you up well to engage with the notebooks, and the slides have frequent code snippets throughout to link theory to code.\n\nFair warning: this workshop will **move fast** and aims to cover a lot of ground in a short space of time. Come to it as a **collaborative challenge**! Together we are going to see what we can get through in a very compressed amount of time. It is **not expected** that you leave the workshop with full understanding of the field of deep learning, a working memory of each architecture or concept you might need in your own research, and complete fluency with PyTorch and Transformers libraries. Rather **success** looks like you having a better understanding of some of the core concepts of deep learning - what it is, what it affords, how it works, when it doesn't (and why) - and knowing where to look yourself to reinforce, deepen and extend from there.\n\n---\n\n**What is already here, and what we expect.** \n\nThe workshop carries no credit and no assessment: every exercise is optional and there to help you deepen your understanding and/or support with your thesis work.\n\n- **Slides and handouts**\n    - These go up before each session.\n    - A handout is the slides plus a full written explanation of each one.\n    - Reading the handout/slides beforehand is, of course, the best preparation you can do, but it is not required or expected - the workshop is self-contained and requires no mandatory preparation.\n    - That said, we will move fast through complex topics, so any preparation you do beforehand will only be a good thing.\n    - I will try to get materials uploaded as early as possible - but please be aware they are WIP and will continue to develop up until the workshop proper. Everything uploaded beforehand is not final content.\n- **The self-study lab series**\n    - These are uploaded from the start: they are last year's labs with tweaks and additional explanation to support self-guided study. They will also be refined and tweaked throughout November/December; I have uploaded what I have now, and will work to further improve and develop them.\n    - There are ten notebooks, covering the whole semester course.\n    - Use them to go further on any topic, at your own pace.\n- **Questions** - shoot me an email, or open an issue in the course's materials repo.",
